    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <h2 style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold;">Installing and commissioning</h2>

        <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">Notice</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">At the point of the vessel wall where the temperature is to be measured, there must be a plane seat surface for the sensor element.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">Procedure</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Screw the welding collar off the connection housing with the sensor element before welding it to the wall.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Position the welding collar with the two threaded holes in such a way that the cable gland on the housing will have the appropriate position later on.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The welding collar must be welded to the wall completely. Only then protection class IP 66 can be ensured.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-top: 20px;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">Mounting</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">Two screws will be visible when the housing cap is screwed off (see Fig. 3-1). These screws have to be loosened to detach the welding collar from the housing with the sensor element.</p>
            <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.4;">The helical spring at the Sensor element will press the element firmly to the wall after mounting. Adding a heat-conductive paste will reduce the half-value time from approximately 20 seconds to 10 seconds.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

